   "text": "I read this as a rifamycin-type ansa macrolactam fused to a phenoxazine, and wrote the SMILES in four parts.\n\n**Core.** The aromatic ring carries an aryl C\u2013O to the furanone, a methyl, and an OH. It is fused to a naphthoquinone-like ring containing a C=O, a C\u2013NH and a C\u2013O carbon.\n\n**Phenoxazine.** The C=N and the ring oxygen close the oxazine ring, which is fused to a benzo ring. The benzo ring bears a 2-[N-methyl-N-(2-pyridyl)amino]ethoxy substituent. In the drawing this ring has the N-bearing carbon, then two CH, then the carbon with the ether, then a CH, then the O-bearing carbon.\n\n**Ansa chain.** From the amide NH the chain runs: C(=O), a trisubstituted Z-alkene with a methyl, an E-alkene, then CH(Me), CH(OH), CH(Me), CH(OH), CH(Me), CH(OAc), CH(Me), CH(OMe), and the enol ether CH=CH\u2013O. The enol ether closes onto the ketal carbon bearing the methyl.\n\n**Stereochemistry.** The wedges and hashes and the alkene geometry in the drawing match the natural rifamycin configuration. I copied the stereo descriptors from the known rifampicin SMILES as I remember it, rather than deriving each centre from the wedges myself, so those descriptors are the least certain part.\n\nSMILES: CO[C@H]1/C=C/O[C@@]2(C)Oc3c(C)c(O)c4c(c3C2=O)C5=Nc6ccc(OCCN(C)c7ccccn7)cc6OC5=C(NC(=O)/C(C)=C\\C=C\\[C@H](C)[C@H](O)[C@@H](C)[C@@H](O)[C@@H](C)[C@H](OC(C)=O)[C@@H]1C)C4=O"
